ducts and have  prohibited the production safe  transportation being excluded from the  conference didn't go over well with  India's tobacco farmers after just a few  minutes of protests outside the  convention 500 farmers were corralled by  police and detained inside this local  police station after a brief negotiation  and a bribe offered from an unnamed  source outside of Reason TV leaders of  the movement were temporarily released  to speak with us outside the walls of  the police station we were planning to  have our own meeting we are protesting  to save our livelihoods web CTC process  is going on in a very undemocratic  manner we have been representing to the  government to consider our pleas not to  go excessive on the regulations because  it's our livelihoods at stake but  unfortunately it's not being done as the  farmers were being released from jail  that evening convention delegates voted  to expel the media from the remainder of  the conference  that's when drew Johnson took a stand by  staying seated I got asked to leave I  did not and ultimately a group of a  group of security guards physically  removed me pulled me out of the building  and this is a large ballroom there are  1,500 delegates it was quite a scene as  you can imagine but I thought it was  completely inappropriate that the media  was kicked out because this is  ultimately a taxpayer-funded event of  people making legislation that could  impact the lives of six billion people  90 percent of the people on earth and  there's no  accountability there's no transparency  so when the media were kicked out I  decided the next morning I would go in  and try to do my job as a journalist and  write a story about what was going on  that day do you swear that the testimony  you're about to give is the truth the  whole truth and nothing but the truth if  it's hard to understand why a four  billion dollar organization like the  w-h-o feels threatened by both  journalists and the average Indian  farmer